**Test adequacy: the content filter is never exercised.**

Mutant: replace `path.is_match(&file) && text.split_terminator('\n').any(|line| content.is_match(line))` with `path.is_match(&file)`. Every test in `tools/hooks-rs/tests/convention_spot_check.rs` still passes.

Why no test catches it: every dirty file that matches a rule's path regex also contains `forbidden_call`. That covers `src/new.ts`, `src/a.ts` (modified) and `src/staged.ts`; `lib/out.ts` matches the `^lib/` rule's content as well. The non-violating `src/` files are never changed, so they never reach the check. Half of the hook's contract (warn only when the content matches) is untested. `tests/hooks/stop-hooks.test.sh` runs the shell hook, not the binary, so it does not cover this either.

A test along these lines would catch it (adjust it to the file's `Fixture` API):

```rust
#[test]
fn file_matching_the_path_but_not_the_content_is_silent() {
    let fixture = Fixture::new("content-miss");
    write(&fixture.repo.join("src/new.ts"), "allowed_call(1);\n");
    assert_silent(&fixture.spot_check("{}"));
}
```

